#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pickup — find a work-in-progress claude/* branch and hand it back to the session that
worked on it. The engine behind /pickup (skills/pickup/SKILL.md); the skill does the fuzzy
part — matching the owner's vague description to one branch, and asking when it can't.

  pickup [list] [--all | --repo PATH] [--json] [--no-fetch]
        every open claude/* branch, local or only on origin, most recently active first:
        PR, worktree, the sessions that worked on it, what was last asked — then one line
        for what launched or closed. Each session line ends `→ <status> (<rule>)`: done, done —
        read the note, waiting on the owner, blocked, running, or unsure when no rule fires;
        `guarded — open it` for the guarded repos (the core's `regatta/guarded`), transcripts, the ~/projects root
        and outside it. Code alone decides, bin/session_status.py (the sessions bench's D2,
        D5, D6: docs/record/jev-sessions-bench-2026-10-02.md); no model until week two clears H2
  pickup show REF [--repo PATH] [--json] [--no-fetch]
        one branch in full, and the handoff `open` would make
  pickup open REF [--repo PATH] [--session ID] [--vscode | --terminal] [--reopen | --fresh |
                  --here | --new-tab | --transcript] [--note TEXT] [--account auto|LABEL
                  [--force]] [--dry-run] [--no-fetch]
        make the branch resumable and hand it over (see "Handoff" below). --account: the
        login a fresh Terminal window runs on — auto (the default) is `bin/account pick`, with
        a seed brief's `kind:` / `provider:` lines; a VS Code start is always the primary's,
        so a named secondary is refused there; a label also applies to a reopened Terminal session, and
        is refused past its own limits unless --force
  pickup review REF [--repo PATH] [--no-fetch]
        a probably-launched branch, for the owner's call (coding-on-workflow D6): `show`, then why it
        counts as probably launched — each commit's equivalent on main or none, each file as
        identical on main / edited on main since / differs — what archiving would lose (the
        differing lines, capped at 40, and open items), and the two exits
  pickup archive REF --confirmed [--repo PATH] [--no-fetch]
        The owner's exit: archive the branch with the reason branches.CONFIRMED
        (refs/archive/…, pruned after seven days; its clean worktree and its GitHub copy go).
        A seed no session has started is accepted too, with SEED_DROPPED as its reason.
        --confirmed records the owner's confirmation, so the checkpoint hook refuses it to agents.
  pickup seed TOPIC --file BRIEF [--repo PATH] [--start | --no-start] [--account auto|LABEL]
                  [--no-blueprint] [--routing THREAD…] [--dry-run] [--no-fetch]
        a follow-up for a session that does not exist yet (see "Seeds" below). A doc the brief names
        that has a spacesheep page gets its `Routed:` line in the seed's commit, after keel's gate
        (`keel routed check`) passes on its page — refused while a thread there is unreflected, bar
        the threads --routing names (a seed started from a comment routes its own thread).
  pickup seed TOPIC --from SCOPE --repo PATH --file BRIEF [--no-start] [--account auto|LABEL]
                  [--no-blueprint] [--dry-run] [--no-fetch]
        the one write out of a confidential scope: the brief reviewed first (see "Out of a
        confidential scope" below)
  pickup brief BRANCH --text T|- [--repo PATH] [--dry-run] [--no-fetch] [--unroute]
        a handoff brief committed onto an EXISTING claude/* branch that no session is writing in, by
        git plumbing — no checkout, so a launchd job with no worktree can hand `dispatch` a run
        (the Lookout and Hail design, S4). Fast-forwards the local branch from origin first; refuses
        a diverged one, a dirty worktree and one `held_by_other` (owner.json under 2 h, not handed
        over); then pushes (the comment above cmd_brief says why). A paged doc the brief names gets
        its `Routed:` line in the same commit, ungated; --unroute takes it out (a released start)
  pickup seeds [--mine] [--open] [--all | --repo PATH] [--dry-run] [--no-fetch]
        the seeds no session has started; --mine: this session's; --open: a tab for each
  pickup next PROMPT|- [--tab] [--dry-run]
        a stage hands over to the next (see "Next" below): `continue` under ~120k context;
        else, in a repo dispatch takes (D1, 2026-10-05), `seeded` — PROMPT committed as the
        branch's brief and the stage started unattended by `dispatch --next`; elsewhere `tab` —
        this session's lock marked handed over and a fresh VS Code tab pre-filled with
        `/pickup '<branch>' --note PROMPT --fresh` — one Enter
  pickup move [REF] --to HOST [--prepare] [--note TEXT] [--dry-run] [--no-fetch]
  pickup move REF --from HOST [--note TEXT] [--dry-run] [--no-fetch]
  pickup move [REF] --cancel | pickup move --check ID
        the conversation goes with its branch to the other machine, the source stays behind
        marked (see "Move" below); REF defaults to the branch this directory is on; HOST is
        a peer `wharf-runs/ssh` names (`home` is the dispatch host) or the laptop (`laptop`), or `cloud`: `--to cloud` and
        `REF --from cloud`, where REF may also be a claude.ai/code link or a session_… id
        (see "The cloud" below). The skill is /move (skills/move/SKILL.md).

The other machine (docs/record/shipping-and-pickup-2026-09-25.md, D6 and D7). A branch whose work
may be elsewhere — made on the other host (`claude/<peer>/…` read from the laptop), naming a
session in a commit that has no transcript here, or with no session here at all — is also
read on every other machine in PEERS, over ssh, by `show` and `open`, whatever host its name
carries (peers_for). Per machine, one read-only probe — a Python script piped to `python3 -` — returns the branch's
worktree on that machine, its uncommitted files, the Claude sessions running there that hold
it, and where that session's transcript lives. `show` and `open` warn from it
(`⚠ ALIVE on <peer> …`, with the line that ends that session; `⚠ N uncommitted file(s) …
not on origin`) and never block: the handoff is still the owner's call. `--transcript` then copies
the session's transcript here as a FORK — new id, the other machine's paths rewritten to this
one's, its worktree bookkeeping dropped (fork_claude_session.py), stamped `relocated` to the
repo root like an unpark — and hands that over in VS Code or Terminal, so the conversation
itself comes along, not only its prompts. The original there is never touched. Hosts and the
ssh alias that reads each: PEERS, the lines of `wharf-runs/ssh` (PICKUP_PEER_<HOST> adds or overrides one; PICKUP_SSH replaces `ssh` and
PICKUP_HOST this machine's name in the tests). An unreachable peer is one line in `show` and nothing else.

REF: a branch name (origin/ optional), a unique part of one, a PR number (`pr12`, `12`), or
a session id or its prefix.

Where the link from a branch to its session comes from, strongest first:
  - transcripts: every user/assistant record carries `gitBranch`, and `worktree-state`
    records carry `worktreeBranch` (~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<session>.jsonl)
  - the `Claude-Session: <id8>` trailer checkpoint.py puts on every checkpoint commit — the
    only link that crosses machines, since the other Mac's session transcript is not on this one
  - `pr-link` records, the hook's PR body ("session `<id8>`"), and the lock's owner.json

Where a transcript lives (Claude Code 2.1.282, relocateSessionTranscript): it follows its
session's cwd. EnterWorktree MOVES it to the worktree's slug directory and appends a
`relocated` record; ExitWorktree moves it back. A session closed while inside its worktree —
the usual state of work in progress — stays parked in
~/.claude/projects/<repo>--claude-worktrees-<name>/, where the VS Code session list, scoped to
the workspace's first folder (extension 2.1.280, sessionListScopeRoot), never looks. `open`
unparks it with the same move and `relocated` stamp ExitWorktree makes, never for a running
session; Claude Code then re-enters the worktree by itself (restoreWorktreeForResume).

Handoff (`open`), first match wins:
  running now      ~/.claude/sessions/<pid>.json with the pid alive and procStart matching
                   (UTC) → say where it runs and touch nothing: two processes on one transcript
                   interleave their writes
  large (≥ 100k tokens) and not running → stop with the two exits, exit 3, nothing changed;
                   --reopen picks it up as below, --fresh adopts here or opens a tab/window
                   pre-filled with /pickup '<branch>' --fresh (D1,
                   docs/record/pickup-cold-handoff-2026-09-29.md). The size, and whether the prompt
                   cache is still warm, come from the transcript's last usage record; --vscode,
                   --terminal and --transcript name a reopen shape and count as the choice
  transcript here, this session in VS Code → vscode://anthropic.claude-code/open?session=…
                   &prompt=…, which opens it in an editor tab (or reveals the open one). The
                   prompt is only pre-filled (webview setInputText), never sent; an unknown id
                   opens a fresh session with it instead, so it is worded to make sense there too
  transcript here, this session in a terminal → a new Terminal window running
                   `claude --resume <id> --name <name> <prompt>` from the directory whose slug
                   holds the transcript; here the prompt IS sent: it only asks for a summary
  no transcript here (made on the other machine, or swept after 30 days) → --here: this
                   session adopts the branch; the worktree is made (from origin if that is the
                   only copy) and a context bundle printed. If this session already did other
                   work and runs in VS Code → --new-tab: a fresh session pre-filled with
                   `/pickup <branch>`, so two topics never share one conversation

Before a handoff: a worktree for the branch — where the session last had it, else
.claude/worktrees/<topic> — with .env linked; a fast-forward when origin has commits the local
branch lacks (the other machine pushed) and the worktree is clean; and a lock held by a
session that is no longer running is released (checkpoint.py would hold it for 2 h). Never a
force, reset, rebase or push, and `main` is never touched. Tests: bin/test_pickup.sh.

Seeds (docs/record/followup-handoff-2026-09-29.md). Work a session finds that belongs to another
session — another repo, its own design, context that would drag along — is seeded when found
(`/todo handoff`): `seed` commits the brief (≤ 60 lines / 4,000 characters, with a `Task:` line)
as docs/handoff/<date>-<topic>.md on a new claude/<host>/<date>-<topic> branch from local main,
by git plumbing, and pushes it. Beside it goes the handoff as a one-block ship,
docs/blueprints/<date>-<topic>.md (wharf-on-orca S5, B9; `handoff_blueprint`): `provider: claude-code`,
the brief as the block's section, `wharf check` clean — written only when it is, else the brief
stands alone and the seed says why; `--no-blueprint` for a seed a ship's block makes (wharf's own, whose
blueprint exists). The brief stays what `pickup` and `dispatch` read. The commit carries the task as its `Prompt:` (so `list` shows
it as last asked) and a `Seeded-By:` trailer — never `Claude-Session:`, which would link the
seeding session and make `open` pick it up. Then, per the owner's D2 (b+c): in this repo from VS
Code, the pre-filled `/pickup '<branch>' --fresh` tab opens at once (nothing runs until Enter);
anything that would need a Terminal window — another repo, a shell — prints its start line and
opens only with --start. `seeds --mine --open` is /finish's second chance, one tab per seed
this session made that no session has started.

Out of a confidential scope (confidential-reach S5; the owner's D4 B, 2026-10-26: the scan and a Claude
judge, no person's yes). A session inside a confidential scope writes nothing outside it but this:
`seed --from SCOPE` refuses unless the cwd is one of SCOPE's paths (the core's scope_match) and SCOPE is
confidential, and unless --repo is given and names an open repo — in no confidential scope, not the
owner's vault (`lighthouse/vault`), no tenant's (lighthouse's `account`, which fails closed here when it
cannot be read; absent, there is no tenancy to ask). Then, before any write — no object, no ref, no
push — aurora's `scope-pass check SCOPE <file> --judge --json` reads what leaves: the brief, the branch
name and the Seeded-By line (a topic names the node as well as a brief can). Its scan and the gate's
`scope.brief` judge must both say clean, of the very bytes pickup hashed; anything else stops, the
verdict's lines on stderr, inside the node. The seed commit then carries docs/handoff/<date>-<topic>.review
beside the brief: JSON with the brief's sha256, both verdicts, the site, provider, model, cost and time —
what dispatch will ask before it runs a seed of confidential origin (S6). Nothing pushed names the scope
(the manager's rule from the owner's words, 2026-10-26: a confidential scope's name never reaches an open
repo): Seeded-By and the .review's origin carry the core's scope_marker(), `confidential:<8 hex>`, which
only a host's registry reads back (scope_of_marker), never the cwd's repo; a brief or topic naming the
scope is refused before the bundle is built. The run starts by `dispatch --next --origin <marker>` where
dispatch takes the repo, never a tab or a window; elsewhere, and under --no-start, the dispatch line is
printed with its --origin.

Next (docs/subagent-cache-2026-10-04.md D6–D7). Run by a session as its last act of a stage,
after a memo Save: under STAGE_TOKENS (120k, PICKUP_STAGE_TOKENS) of context it prints
`continue` and the session goes on itself; at or above (or with --tab), on a claude/* branch in
VS Code, it marks this session's lock `handover` in owner.json and opens a fresh tab pre-filled
with `/pickup '<branch>' --note '<PROMPT>' --fresh` (`-`: PROMPT from stdin). That tab's `open
--fresh` does not refuse the marking session as running, and takes the lock at once; until
then checkpoint.py lets the next writer take a marked lock, and a prompt typed in the old
session withdraws the mark. Every link goes to a VS Code window of the repo it is for, never
the focused one (vscode_target(), `windowId=`): the calling session's own when it is on that
repo, else an open window there (the extension's IDE lock, vscode_window_on()), else the one
`code <repo>` opens. None identified for sure: a Terminal window instead, saying why (the owner,
2026-10-13: "I don't want to mix things in my repos"). So `open --fresh` on another repo's
branch from VS Code is a tab in that repo's window (`foreign-vscode`), not a Terminal window.

Move (docs/record/teleport-design-2026-10-02.md, E2 and E3; the skill is /move). `--to HOST`: the
gates (on the workflow; nothing of the session still running; the target answers and holds no
dirty worktree or live session on the branch), then checkpoint.py's commit of what is
uncommitted and a push; over ssh the worktree there (made, or fast-forwarded), its .env link
and a gitignored .claude/settings.local.json pinning the source's model and effort; the
transcript forked, cut before the running turn, paths rewritten, its subagents, tool results
and scratchpad carried in one tar; VS Code opened on that worktree with the session in it; the
proof is the target's live registry naming the new id within 60 s, else the Terminal line to
start it. Then the marker here (common.MOVED_FILE), so the session left behind cannot write.
An unreachable target, or the laptop seen from the build Mac (ssh runs one way, D2), is `--prepare`:
commit, push, a pending marker, and the line to type there. `--from HOST` is the return leg:
a busy session there stops it; otherwise that worktree is marked and what it left committed
and pushed, the conversation carried here as `open --transcript` does, the pin written, this
worktree's marker cleared — and the session there is the owner's to end (D4: an agent never ends
one). `--check ID`: the fork's first reply against the source's last, model and effort (R2).

The cloud (the same design, E6). Claude Code's own `/teleport` and `--teleport` cross; `move`
does what surrounds them. `--to cloud`: the same gates, commit and push, a marker `to: cloud`,
then a new Terminal window continuing a fork of this session with `/teleport` as its first prompt
(the fallback `claude --cloud "<brief>"` printed beside it; `--cancel` takes the marker back
if neither reaches the cloud). `REF --from cloud`: the session id read from the newest commit on
the branch carrying its claude.ai/code link (origin's copy first), the worktree here caught up,
`claude -p --teleport ID` writing the local copy, pinned and opened in VS Code as `--from` does
— or, when no copy appears, `claude --teleport ID` in a Terminal window.

Reuses hooks/common.py (host name; GitHub REST with the token git already has — no `gh`),
hooks/branches.py (whether a branch launched: proved, probably, or carried elsewhere — only it
judges that) and fork_claude_session.py (the slug rule, the Terminal opener). Stdlib only,
Python 3.9.
"""
import argparse
import datetime
import fnmatch
import hashlib
import inspect
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import urllib.parse
import uuid
from pathlib import Path

BIN = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(BIN.parent / "lib"))
import core_path  # noqa: E402 — the regatta core (C2); none: one line, exit 2
core_path.require()
import common  # noqa: E402
try:
    from transcript import call, call_line  # noqa: E402 — the core's one test of a call (peer_probe_source ships both)
except ImportError:   # a core older than transcript.py (a plugin install): C4's one line, exit 2
    sys.exit("pickup: the regatta core predates hooks/transcript.py — update the regatta plugin")
# wharf (M2, a hard edge) holds session_status, fork_claude_session (its bin/), branches and
# checkpoint (its hooks/): both folders join the path after the core's, this module's bin/ first.
for _peer in ("branches.py", "session_status.py"):
    _p = common.tool(_peer)
    if not _p:
        sys.exit("pickup: wharf's %s not found — install the `wharf` module beside wharf-runs" % _peer)
    sys.path.insert(2, str(_p.parent))
sys.path.insert(0, str(BIN))
# PICKUP_HOST stands in for this machine's name, as AUTOFINISH_HOST and DISPATCH_HOST do. The
# suite is written from the laptop with the build Mac as the ssh peer; run on the build Mac, the real
# name dropped that peer from PEERS and 26 peer cases failed (2026-10-08). Seeded into common's cache,
# so what pickup imports (checkpoint's branch names, fork_claude_session's forked-from) agrees.
if os.environ.get("PICKUP_HOST"):
    common._host = re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9-]", "-", os.environ["PICKUP_HOST"].lower()).strip("-") or "host"
import session_status as rules  # noqa: E402
from fork_claude_session import IDE_BLOCK, announce, fork_transcript, open_in_terminal, slug  # noqa: E402

CONFIG = Path(os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR") or Path.home() / ".claude")
PROJECTS = CONFIG / "projects"
REGISTRY = CONFIG / "sessions"
ARCHIVE = Path(os.environ.get("CLAUDE_TRANSCRIPT_ARCHIVE", "~/projects/transcripts")).expanduser()
# Text addressed to the owner names them as the core's `regatta/owner` key gives it; unset, "the owner"
OWNER = common.OWNER_NAME or "the owner"
OWNER_TTL = common.OWNER_TTL  # checkpoint.py's: an owner idle this long has gone anyway
# archive's reason for a seed nobody wants: it never launched, so CONFIRMED's words would lie
# (followup-handoff review, 2026-09-29; the default applied 2026-10-11, built 2026-10-19)
SEED_DROPPED = f"Unstarted seed, dropped by {OWNER}"
SURVEY_SECONDS = 10   # hooks/branches.py's proof, bounded: what it can't finish stays a candidate
# A branch's status words that say it is on main: `launched` since rename S5 switched pickup's writer,
# `landed` before it (docs/wharf-rename-2026-10-16.md, M13); every compare takes both
LAUNCHED = ("landed", "launched")
PROBABLY = ("probably landed", "probably launched")
SCHEME = os.environ.get("PICKUP_VSCODE_SCHEME", "vscode")
# tests: everything but opening a window; PICKUP_NO_LAUNCH is its old name, read until S8 of
# docs/wharf-rename-2026-10-16.md drops it ("launch" is the release verb now)
NO_START = bool(os.environ.get("PICKUP_NO_START") or os.environ.get("PICKUP_NO_LAUNCH"))
# A large session is handed over as a choice, reopen or fresh (D1, 2026-09-29; the numbers
# behind it: docs/record/pickup-cold-handoff-2026-09-29.md). 94% of real sessions are above the line.
FRESH_TOKENS = 100_000
FRESH_BASE = 60_000     # a fresh session's first call, when this repo has none to measure
CALLS_PER_TURN = 30     # API calls per human prompt: p50 33 over 105 sessions (2026-09-29)
WARM_MARGIN = 120       # seconds for the owner to press Enter: the band before expiry counts as cold
CONTEXT_KEYS = ("ctx", "ctx_at", "ctx_end", "ttl", "first_ctx")
USAGE_NUM = re.compile(r'"(input_tokens|cache_read_input_tokens|cache_creation_input_tokens'
                       r'|ephemeral_5m_input_tokens|ephemeral_1h_input_tokens)":(\d+)')
META = ("ai-title", "custom-title", "relocated", "worktree-state", "pr-link")
NAME_RE = re.compile(r"^claude/(?:(?P<host>[a-z0-9-]+)/)?(?P<date>\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})-(?P<topic>.+)$")
GIT_BRANCH = re.compile(r'"gitBranch":"([^"]*)"')
STAMP = re.compile(r'"timestamp":"([^"]+)"')
ENTRY = re.compile(r'"entrypoint":"([^"]+)"')
CWD = re.compile(r'"cwd":"([^"]+)"')
# Tool calls, read off the raw line like gitBranch: a call's id in the assistant record, its
# result's in the next user record (ids are the API's `toolu_…`; inside a string they are escaped)
TOOL_USE_ID = re.compile(r'"id":"(toolu_[A-Za-z0-9_-]+)"')
TOOL_RESULT_ID = re.compile(r'"tool_use_id":"(toolu_[A-Za-z0-9_-]+)"')
SCANNED = {}  # path → scan(), so a transcript found twice (a repo's own, a trailer's) is read once
COMMAND = re.compile(r"<command-name>(?P<name>[^<]*)</command-name>"
                     r"(?:.*?<command-args>(?P<args>.*?)</command-args>)?", re.S)
PROMPT_HEAD = re.compile(r"^(?:Prompt|Prompts \(oldest first\)):\s*$", re.M)
HOOK_PR = re.compile(r"Agent work in progress on \*\*(?P<host>[^*]+)\*\*, session `(?P<sid>[0-9a-f]+)`")
# Seeds (docs/record/followup-handoff-2026-09-29.md): the brief a session writes for a follow-up that
# earns its own session. The caps keep it a brief — the fresh session reads it whole, and a
# design belongs in docs/ with a two-line brief pointing at it.
# What a seed commit is lives in hooks/branches.py, which reaps the empty ones (R7 of
# docs/record/branch-reaping-2026-10-04.md: one definition, so the list and the reaper never disagree).
from branches import BLUEPRINT_DIR, HANDOFF_DIR, SEED_SUBJECT, SEEDED_BY_RE as SEEDED_BY, seed_paths_ok  # noqa: E402
BRIEF_LINES, BRIEF_CHARS = 60, 4000
TOPIC_RE = re.compile(r"^(?!.*-raw$)[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{2,39}$")  # -raw: /finish's backups, skipped everywhere
# machine → the ssh alias this one reads it with: the lines of `wharf-runs/ssh` (`<peer>:<alias>`, the
# peer lower-cased, as dispatch reads them); every entry but this machine is asked. The build Mac has
# no alias for the laptop (it sleeps), so a key that names only the build Mac runs laptop → build Mac only.
def _ssh_peers():
    out = {}
    for line in common.config_lines("wharf-runs/ssh") or []:
        host, _, alias = line.partition(":")
        host, alias = host.strip().lower(), alias.strip()
        if host and alias and not host.startswith("#"):
            out[host] = alias
    return out


PEERS = _ssh_peers()
_dispatch_noted = []


def dispatch_host():
    """The Mac runs are dispatched to: `wharf-runs/dispatch-host`, else the first peer `wharf-runs/ssh`
    names (one stderr line saying so), else "" (every run stays here)."""
    v = common.config_line("wharf-runs/dispatch-host").lower()
    if v or not PEERS:
        return v
    if not _dispatch_noted:
        _dispatch_noted.append(1)
        print("pickup: no wharf-runs/dispatch-host key — taking the first peer of wharf-runs/ssh as the dispatch host",
              file=sys.stderr)
    return next(iter(PEERS))


def dispatch_where():
    """For a message: where dispatch starts a run, the laptop when it does not answer."""
    return f"on {dispatch_host() or 'the dispatch host'}, the laptop when it does not answer"


def a_peer():
    """A peer to name in a hint: the first other Mac the key holds, else the laptop, else a placeholder."""
    me = common.host_name()
    return next((h for h in PEERS if h != me), "") or (common.LAPTOP if common.LAPTOP != me else "<host>")
SSH = os.environ.get("PICKUP_SSH") or "ssh"
# The probe the other machine runs: stdin to `python3 -`, argv = repo path under ~/projects, the
# branch, the session-id prefixes its commits name. Reads only; prints one JSON object.
PEER_PROBE = r'''
import glob, json, os, subprocess, sys
from pathlib import Path
rel, branch, prefixes = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], [p for p in sys.argv[3].split(",") if p]
home = Path.home()
repo = home / "projects" / rel
def run(*a):
    try:
        return subprocess.run(a, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=15).stdout
    except Exception:
        return ""
out = {"home": str(home), "worktree": None, "dirty": [], "lock": None, "moved": None, "sessions": [], "transcripts": {}}
for block in run("git", "-C", str(repo), "worktree", "list", "--porcelain").split("\n\n"):
    lines = block.splitlines()
    if lines and lines[0].startswith("worktree ") and "branch refs/heads/" + branch in lines:
        out["worktree"] = lines[0][len("worktree "):]
if out["worktree"]:
    out["dirty"] = [l[3:] for l in run("git", "-C", out["worktree"], "--no-optional-locks", "status",
                                       "--porcelain").splitlines() if l.strip()]  # never writes its index
    gd = run("git", "-C", out["worktree"], "rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir").strip()
    try:
        out["lock"] = json.loads(Path(gd, "claude", "owner.json").read_text())
    except Exception:
        pass
    try:  # the /move marker (common.MOVED_FILE): read raw here, worded by bin/pickup
        out["moved"] = json.loads(Path(gd, "claude", "teleported.json").read_text())
    except Exception:
        pass
for f in (home / ".claude" / "sessions").glob("*.json"):
    try:
        e = json.loads(f.read_text())
        os.kill(int(e["pid"]), 0)
    except Exception:
        continue
    out["sessions"].append({k: e.get(k) for k in ("pid", "sessionId", "cwd", "status", "statusUpdatedAt",
                                                  "entrypoint", "name")})
for p in prefixes:
    hits = sorted(glob.glob(str(home / ".claude" / "projects" / "*" / (p + "*.jsonl"))))
    hits += sorted(glob.glob(str(home / "projects" / "transcripts" / Path(rel).name / ("*-" + p[:8] + ".jsonl"))))
    if hits:
        out["transcripts"][p] = hits[0]
if not out["transcripts"]:
    # No trailer named a session: the newest transcript with records on the branch, in any
    # project's folder (a sibling repo's session commits here too). 34 MB, 0.2 s on the build Mac.
    top = home / ".claude" / "projects"
    found = [f for f in run("grep", "-rlF", "--include=*.jsonl", '"gitBranch":"' + branch + '"', str(top)).splitlines()
             if Path(f).parent.parent == top]  # a session's own file, never its subagents/
    if found:
        out["transcripts"]["branch"] = max(found, key=lambda f: os.path.getmtime(f))
def context_of(f):  # context_step and context_done come from bin/pickup (peer_probe_source)
    st = {}
    try:
        with open(f, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
